President Trump is welcoming North  Korea's apparent openness to  negotiations about its nuclear program  North Korean leader Kim jong-un  reportedly told South Korean officials  this week that he's willing to discuss  giving up his country's nuclear weapons  and suspending its nuclear program  during any talks president Trump called  the statements very positive it's not  yet clear whether the you asked would  meet the North Koreans Republican  senator Cory Gardner of Colorado is on  the Foreign Relations Committee he  chairs the Subcommittee on East Asia the  Pacific and international cybersecurity  policies with us from Capitol Hill for  an interview you'll only see on CBS this  morning senator good morning what do you  make of North Korea's position are they  reacting to sanctions are they pausing  to reload or is this a part of a long  pattern of kind of cat-and-mouse there  is no doubt that the sanctions that have  been put in place against the Kim regime  are starting to work I've talked to  officials at the CIA the State  Department they're starting to feel the  pressure from significant sanctions not  only from the United States but from our  allies and partners around the globe but  we've also been here before and so we  have to take this certainly it's  positive but we have to take this sign  and this gesture to talk not only with a  grain of salt but with perhaps an entire  salt block so senator what is the next  move as you'd advise for the  administration in terms of not being  fooled again from what the North Koreans  have done before in the past the United  States has lived up to our end of the  bargain where we've entered into  promises or agreements with North Korea  only to see the North Koreans break  their end of the bargain and so what  this administration needs to continue to  do is apply maximum pressure as it has  continuing to engage China and other  countries around the world to enforce  stricter standards in stricter sanctions  to make sure the United Nations  continues to put pressure on them and  then go after the the heinous that  heinous slave labor practices of the  North Korean regime to cut off even more  dollars that go back to prop up kim  jeong-hoon so there's plenty of action  that can be taken and this  administration needs to take every  single one of them let me ask you the  South Korean leader that met with Kim  jong-un said that he received a pledge  from Kim jong-un that  would halt testing if during these  negotiations do you believe them when  they say that well if they halt testing  that doesn't mean that they halt their  progress I think they've reached the  stage and their nuclear program we're  sure they can say look we're not gonna  we're not gonna test but in the meantime  they continue to develop supply lines  develop new technologies testing through  computer models it doesn't mean that  they give up or completely halt their  nuclear program that's a big difference  but I think it's important to go back to  what they have said they would do in the  past they've actually said they would do  more in terms of agreeing with us to  give up their nuclear program this is  one of their lesser promises they've  made and so we must continue maximum  pressure and not give up on our desire  our demand of complete verifiable  irreversible denuclearization  so how optimistic are you senator you  tweeted yesterday that we need to make  sure I'm paraphrasing that we avoid the  mistakes we made in the past you feeling  optimistic and what mistakes where are  you referring to  well I again the mistakes that we have  made in terms of believing that kid the  the Kim family his father his  grandfather was serious about the  actions that he was going to take  there's no doubt that we have to have a  resolution and again I agree this is a  positive development but will that  positive development that lead to  concrete results of denuclearization and  that's what we have to have so I am  skeptical this is a good thing but we  have to make sure that we're not fooled  as the old saying goes and not fooled  twice shame on us what do you make of  the resignation of Gary cone yesterday  from the White House what do you make of  that and the president has tweeted  there's no chaos here yeah I think the  op tempo of the White House as you know  is incredibly different than many other  White House administrations I think this  is probably something that Gary has  thought about long and hard over the  past several months as he obviously has  different things that he could be doing  in the private sector I look forward to  sitting down and talking with him about  how we can continue to work with the  administration on trade agreements that  are in his remaining time trade  agreements that are fair to the American  people but also don't punish the  American people through a punitive trade  actions but the precipitating event in  this case was the president's steel  tariffs you are in charge of getting  Republicans elected most Republicans  don't agree with those tariffs how do  they and Paul Ryan has said  tariffs are gonna walk back some of the  gains from the tax cut how do you sell  Republicans to the public when on the  one hand taxes are helping them but in  this case according to Paul Ryan it's  hurting them well there's no doubt that  the president campaigned on this issue  so there's not a surprise to anyone who  was following the campaign that the  president made this decision and I think  he's right to say we need trade  agreements they're fair where I disagree  with of course is on the decision to  impose a tariff on steel aluminum or to  withdraw from NAFTA that could actually  hurt the American people I represent a  state that has a lot of great beer  industries a lot of farmers who are very  worried about the impact steel aluminum  tariffs could have on them trade deals  like NAFTA very important to them so  let's make sure they're fair let's make  sure they're good for the American  people but if we take the wrong economic  policies and I think imposing a tariff  is then it could lead to higher costs on  the American consumer it could cost jobs  instead of saving jobs and that's not  the right step so I look forward to  continuing to work with the White House  to make sure that we do the right thing  and not something that could hurt all  right senator Gardner thanks so much for  being us
 

